  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1216 PM MDT Sat Oct 24 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\nThe storm system that produced the widespread rain/snow on Friday\nhas exited the area already this morning. In its wake, mainly some\nlow clouds, along with patchy fog, but much cooler temperatures\nhave also moved into the region. The fog should thin out by late\nmorning, otherwise expect below normal temperatures this\nafternoon.\n\nThe below normal temperatures are likely to last most of the week\nfor much of the region.\n\nThe next weather system arrives Monday night into Tuesday, as an\nupper level low approaches from the west. At this time,\nprecipitation amounts look to be a bit lighter than this past\nweather system.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\nThe main concern with the next weather system will once again be\nwhen does the rain change to snow. The airmass will be cooler, so\nit is likely that snow levels will start out quite a bit lower.\nThe main question is will the rain to change all the way down to\nplains/valley floors? The forecast models vary, with some models\nkeeping the snow level above 4500 feet, thus rain falling at the\nlowest elevations. Also, precipitation amounts should be lighter\nwith this system, thus right now it looks like the highest\npotential for any winter statements will mainly be in the mountain\nareas south of I-90 from Butte to Bozeman. Brusda",
